window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-10-20T04:40:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-10-19","event":"Q3 GDP and September activity (printed); Financial Street Forum opening (printed)","why":"Printed: GDP 4.5% y/y and 1.2% q/q, industrial production 5.5%, retail sales 1.1%, FAI −7.2% with property −20.6%, unemployment 5.2%, home prices −2.8% y/y; Pan Gongsheng flagged the overnight rate as the main policy rate, a bond-market backstop for non-banks and five opening measures; Wu Qing approved 18 active ETFs"},{"date":"2026-10-20","event":"LPR fixing (printed); PBoC overnight repo announcement (printed); Hong Kong reopens after Chung Yeung","why":"Printed: 1-year 3.00% and 5-year 3.50% unchanged for a 17th month; zero seven-day operation against CNY 65B maturing; daily overnight reverse repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct to 2 Nov; Hang Seng about 1% lower near 23,960 at midday"},{"date":"2026-10-21","event":"First day of the PBoC overnight reverse repo window (21 Oct to 2 Nov, up to CNY 1T a day); Financial Street Forum continues","why":"The size and rate of the first operation show whether the 1.25% overnight rate is the de facto anchor Huatai and ANZ describe; CNY 56B of seven-day repos mature"},{"date":"2026-10-22","event":"Financial Street Forum closing; SAFE session","why":"SAFE promised incremental current-account and capital-account measures on 19 Oct; official remarks on FX management with the fixing at 6.7200 and the onshore yuan at 6.6817, its firmest since July 2022"},{"date":"2026-10-23","event":"CNY 25B 30-year CGB auction","why":"First long-bond supply after the 30-year active bond fell to 2.055% on 16 Oct and backed up to 2.069% on 19 Oct; Guosheng targets below 2.0%"},{"date":"2026-10-26","event":"Fifth Plenum of the 20th Central Committee, 26 to 29 Oct","why":"Party governance ahead of the 2027 congress; not an economic plenum"},{"date":"2026-10-26","event":"October tax payment deadline (moved to 26 Oct)","why":"Changjiang Fixed Income notes the later deadline concentrates funding pressure in the last week of October, the test for whether the CNY 400B of net medium-term liquidity added in October holds DR007 below 1.40%"},{"date":"2026-10-27","event":"September industrial profits","why":"Prior +15.7% YTD; the first read on whether PPI at 5.0% reaches margins or squeezes them"},{"date":"2026-10-28","event":"FOMC decision (US), 27 to 28 Oct","why":"Warsh gave no steer on 16 Oct; the US 10-year fell to 5.08% on 15 Oct and the dollar index to 102.055, so the pressure on the yuan and Asian tech eased for now"},{"date":"2026-10-29","event":"US FCC vote on barring China-based labs from testing US-bound electronics","why":"82% of covered devices were tested in China in 2025; the ban would start in December 2028 and tests how durable the tariff truce is"},{"date":"2026-10-31","event":"October NBS PMIs","why":"Manufacturing prior 50.1 (Trading Economics forecast 50.3), non-manufacturing 50.2; does construction hold above 50 after the subsidy"},{"date":"2026-11-02","event":"October RatingDog manufacturing PMI","why":"Prior 52.1, tenth month of expansion"},{"date":"2026-11-04","event":"October RatingDog services PMI","why":"Prior 51.6; whether services firms keep cutting selling prices"},{"date":"2026-11-07","event":"October FX reserves","why":"Prior $3.400T after a $38.1B fall in September; gold holdings have risen for 23 months"},{"date":"2026-11-09","event":"October CPI and PPI","why":"Prior CPI 0.9%, PPI 5.0%; whether oil keeps PPI near 5% and whether core CPI moves off 1.0%"},{"date":"2026-11-10","event":"October trade","why":"Prior exports +36.3%, imports +31.0%, surplus $133.5B; no consensus yet"},{"date":"2026-11-13","event":"October credit: TSF, new loans, M2 (release window 10 to 15 Nov)","why":"Prior TSF 7.1%, M2 7.2%, loans 4.8%; whether the CNY 700B outright reverse repo and the September package lift private borrowing"},{"date":"2026-11-15","event":"Comment period closes on the MOFCOM/MOST draft technology export catalogue (40 technologies: 23 AI, 12 PV, 5 biomedicine)","why":"A final version follows; the temporary registration requirement for technology exports already applies from 16 Oct and the US response under the truce is the open question"},{"date":"2026-11-16","event":"October industrial production, retail sales, FAI, unemployment; 70-city home prices","why":"NBS calendar: 16 Nov, 10:00 Beijing; second month after the 1 Oct mortgage subsidy"}],"cb":{"label":"Easing","note":"The LPR was held on 20 Oct for a 17th month (1-year 3.00%, 5-year 3.50%) and the 7-day rate stays 1.40%, but Pan Gongsheng said on 19 Oct the PBoC will study making the overnight repo (new issues at 1.25%, DR001 about 1.26%) its main policy rate, which Huatai calls a de facto cut, announced a bond-market liquidity backstop for non-bank redemptions, and the PBoC scheduled daily overnight reverse repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct to 2 Nov."},"cbName":"PBoC stance","conflicts":[{"a":"Q3 GDP 4.5% (consensus 4.4%), September retail sales 1.1%, industrial production 5.5%, unemployment 5.2%, exports +36.3% and PMIs above 50","b":"FAI −7.2% year to date with property −20.6% and private investment −10.4%, TSF 7.1%, M2 7.2%, M1 3.2%, consumer confidence 89.5, car retail sales −28% and the NBS calling domestic demand \"insufficient\"","read":"The coincident data beat on exports, subsidies and a low base, and the quarter landed at the bottom of the target range. The leading and credit data say the beat is not self-sustaining: new orders flat at 50.5, households not borrowing and investment still contracting outside manufacturing. The framework puts the leading set first, so the regime stays Slowdown with an improving coincident set rather than moving to Recovery; Eswar Prasad calls reliance on foreign demand \"untenable\" and ANZ says the first nine months intensified concerns about imbalance.","trigger":"October NBS PMIs on 31 Oct (prior 50.1 and 50.2): new orders above 51 and non-manufacturing holding 50 would move the regime toward Recovery; a slip below 50 after the subsidy month would confirm Slowdown. October credit in mid-November with TSF above 7.1% would be the first acceleration since mid-2025."},{"a":"PPI at +5.0%, the highest since June 2022, industrial purchasing prices +6.9% y/y and Brent near $109.6","b":"CPI at 0.9%, core CPI at 1.0%, 70-city prices −2.8%, M1 growth 3.2% and the 10-year CGB at 1.645%","read":"Producer prices are pushed up by oil and coal and purchasing prices are rising faster than output prices, so the squeeze sits in downstream margins rather than in demand; the 4.1-point PPI-CPI gap is the widest since 2021. The bond market still trades the deflation side, backing up only 0.75 bp on a GDP beat, and the PBoC is moving toward a lower overnight anchor rather than tightening into PPI.","trigger":"September industrial profits on 27 Oct (prior +15.7% year to date) show whether the cost push reaches margins; October CPI and PPI on 9 Nov with PPI above 4% and core below 1% would keep the squeeze."},{"a":"Home prices −2.8% y/y in September after −3.0%, the smallest fall in nine months; tier-1 new homes −0.5% y/y and rising m/m for a second month; second-hand online transactions +10.7% year to date","b":"Property investment −20.6% year to date (from −19.9%), new-home sales −13.2% by area and −13.9% by value, iron ore at $90.95 (−6.7% in a month) and property stocks falling on the mainland on 19 Oct","read":"Prices in the largest cities are stabilising on the Shanghai easing and the 1 Oct mortgage subsidy, while volumes and investment keep falling, so the price signal leads and the activity signal lags. The NBS says the market is \"moving in a positive direction\"; S&P's base case still puts the national trough in Q3 2028. For local-government finances and commodity demand the investment line matters more than the price line, and it worsened.","trigger":"October top-100 developer sales in early November and October 70-city prices on 16 Nov; a second month of tier-1 m/m gains with a narrower investment decline would change the read, and iron ore back above $95 would confirm it."},{"a":"Yuan at 6.6817, its firmest close since July 2022, the 10-year CGB at 1.645%, copper cash at $14,496 and a $133.5B September surplus","b":"CSI 300 at 4,174.53, below the 4,200 line for a third session despite a GDP beat; ChiNext at 2,746.29 (−0.94% on 19 Oct); turnover down to CNY 1.44T; Hang Seng about 1% lower near 23,960 on 20 Oct","read":"The currency, bonds, copper and the surplus price export strength and loose funding; equities price a tech valuation reset imported from the US AI trade plus the removal of the rate-cut hope after the beat. Banks and liquor carried the index on 19 Oct while growth names fell and turnover shrank, which is rotation inside a supported market, not new money; the state support of 15 and 16 Oct (insurer guidance, CNY 16.1B through the main CSI 300 ETF) has not been followed by a close above the reversal level.","trigger":"The posture is Risk-off; a CSI 300 close above 4,217.68 reverses it and a close below 4,115.07 extends it. The 20 Oct Hong Kong close, the 27 Oct profits data and the 28 Oct FOMC are the next tests; a US 10-year above 5.2% would add pressure on Asian tech."},{"a":"Money is loose and getting looser in structure: overnight Shibor 1.264%, 7-day Shibor 1.322% (lowest since May), daily overnight repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct, a study of the overnight rate (1.25%) as the main policy rate, a bond-market backstop for non-banks, PSL 1.50% and CNY 550B of local debt quota","b":"The LPR was held at 3.00% for a 17th month, the 7-day rate stays 1.40%, PPI at 5.0% and a Fed that may hike again argue against a broad cut, and TSF growth slowed for a 14th month to 7.1% with loans at a record-low 4.8%","read":"The PBoC is cutting the effective anchor by changing the instrument rather than the rate: moving the policy rate to the overnight tenor at 1.25% lowers it 15 bp without a headline cut, which fits a 4.5% GDP print that removed the case for one. Cheap money is still not being borrowed, government bonds carry TSF and the IMF says fiscal support is too small, so the credit impulse stays fiscal. The framework change is a Liquidity signal, not a Growth signal, until credit data turn.","trigger":"The PBoC guidance on the tenor switch and the first overnight operations from 21 Oct (size and rate); the 26 Oct tax date is the test of whether DR001 holds near 1.26%; October credit data in mid-November with TSF above 7.1% and loans above 4.8% would show the lower anchor reaching borrowers."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-10-20","impact":"The hold matched every forecast after the 4.5% GDP print and the PBoC said dealer demand for seven-day money on 20 Oct was zero against CNY 65B maturing. The overnight window, announced at 10:56 Beijing and running at a fixed rate with quantity tender, covers the 26 Oct tax date and follows Pan Gongsheng's 19 Oct remark that the overnight repo should become the main policy rate; the overnight rate has been 1.25% on new issues since June and DR001 is about 1.26%. The fixing of 6.7200 was 8 pips firmer and about 390 pips weaker than the 6.6813 estimate. Hong Kong reopened about 1% lower near 23,960 after the Chung Yeung holiday with Lenovo −5.8% and Tencent −2.0%, and the Shanghai Composite was down about 0.5% at midday.","src":"Investing.com; The Paper; China News Service; investinglive; Trading Economics via TradingView","tag":"Policy","title":"LPR held at 3.00% and 3.50% for a 17th month; PBoC runs a zero seven-day operation and schedules daily overnight reverse repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct to 2 Nov; fixing 6.7200","url":"https://www.thepaper.cn/newsDetail_forward_34287766"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"January to September growth is 4.6%, inside the 4.5% to 5.0% target, and nominal GDP CNY 107.1T. Industrial output was the fastest in six months and retail sales the fastest since March, the services production index rose 4.8% and unemployment fell 0.1 point to 5.2%. Fixed-asset investment stayed at −7.2% (forecast −7.3%) with property −20.6%, infrastructure −4.2%, manufacturing −2.1% and private investment −10.4%; new-home sales fell 13.2% by area and 13.9% by value, and exports rose 29.6% in yuan terms. The NBS said domestic demand is \"insufficient\"; Capital Economics said the economy \"regained momentum\" and ANZ said fiscal support remains crucial with monetary easing constrained. Eswar Prasad called reliance on foreign demand \"untenable and unsustainable\".","src":"NBS press release; investinglive; Babypips; ABC News (AP); Business Recorder (Reuters)","tag":"Data","title":"Q3 GDP +4.5% y/y (consensus 4.4%) and +1.2% q/q; September industrial production +5.5%, retail sales +1.1%, FAI −7.2% year to date with property −20.6%, unemployment 5.2%","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202610/t20261019_1965619.html"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"Pan said the PBoC will study switching its main open-market tenor from seven days (1.40%) to overnight (1.25% on new issues), will retire the 2015 benchmark deposit and lending rates, and created a liquidity tool with the NFRA and CSRC for large redemptions at wealth-management subsidiaries, fund managers and their parents, to be used only in systemic stress against high-grade collateral. He widened SLF access to all deposit-takers including foreign banks, said interbank-exchange bond connection is ready pending tests, opened onshore FX trading to eight offshore clearing banks and issued guidance on financing three innovation centres; digital yuan operators rise from 10 to 30. Huatai called the tenor switch a de facto rate cut and ANZ's Xing Zhaopeng said it \"will substantially change the policy framework\". Wu Qing called market stability a top priority, approved the first 18 actively managed ETFs, encouraged buy-backs and major-shareholder purchases, promised more QFII access and Connect additions, and warned of offshore leveraged products on single A-shares; no direct buying facility was announced. SAFE promised current-account easing and NFRA wider foreign access.","src":"Global Times; BigGo Finance; briefs.co; ECNS; ChainCatcher","tag":"Policy","title":"Financial Street Forum: Pan Gongsheng to study the overnight rate as the main policy rate, launches a bond-market backstop for non-bank redemptions and five opening measures; Wu Qing approves 18 active ETFs and urges buy-backs","url":"https://www.globaltimes.cn/page/202610/1372568.shtml"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"Banks led with ICBC and China Construction Bank at intraday records and four banks up more than 3%, liquor and robotics (Unitree +10%) followed, while the STAR 50 fell about 2% and CXMT 7.8% as the AI hardware unwind continued; turnover fell to CNY 1.44T from 1.56T and the margin balance is CNY 2.49T after a CNY 60B weekly fall. CICC puts the CSI 300 at about 13 times earnings and sees no reason for medium-term pessimism; CITIC says the correction ends when institutional holdings regain excess returns. The 30-year bond rose 1.4 bp to 2.069%, overnight Shibor held at 1.264%, 7-day Shibor fell to 1.322% (lowest since May) and the PBoC added CNY 30B net. The yuan closed 51 pips firmer at 6.6817, the strongest since July 2022, with the fixing at 6.7208, 311 pips weaker than the estimate. LME cash copper settled at $14,496 (−0.7%) and iron ore at $90.95; the posture stays Risk-off with 4,217.68 the reversal level.","src":"Tencent Finance klines; Xinhua; 21st Century Business Herald; Eastmoney; FX168; Westmetall; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"19 Oct close: CSI 300 +0.09% to 4,174.53, still below 4,200; Shanghai +0.39% to 3,730.92 as banks hit records; ChiNext −0.94% to 2,746.29; 10-year CGB +0.75 bp to 1.645%; yuan closes at 6.6817; Hong Kong closed","url":"https://english.news.cn/20261019/24bc622feefc4e50be0b1cbb608fc8db/c.html"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"The 39th consecutive annual decline. Tier-1 new-home prices were −0.5% y/y (−0.9% in August) and tier-1 second-hand −1.5% (−2.7%), with tier-1 new prices up m/m for a second month and second-hand for a seventh; Shanghai +2.8% y/y, Shenzhen −1.0% (−2.3%), Guangzhou −1.1% (−1.9%), Beijing −2.7% (−2.3%), Chongqing −2.4% and Tianjin −3.6%. Second-hand prices rose m/m in 9 cities (3 in August). The NBS said the market is \"moving in a positive direction\", but property investment fell 20.6% year to date and new-home sales 13.2% by area; second-hand online transactions rose 10.7%. Property stocks fell on the mainland on 19 Oct.","src":"Xinhua; Reuters via KFGO; FX.co; NBS press release","tag":"Property","title":"70-city new home prices −2.8% y/y in September after −3.0%, the smallest fall in nine months; −0.1% m/m; prices rose m/m in 17 cities; tier-1 new homes −0.5% y/y","url":"https://english.news.cn/20261019/b53a637a2f0f462abb75d08194abc399/c.html"},{"date":"2026-10-19","impact":"Nvidia, AMD and Intel led a rebound from the Nasdaq's worst week since June, consumer discretionary rose 2.7% and technology 1.2% while utilities fell 2.0%; the NAHB housing index fell to 29, the lowest since 2012. The US 10-year rose on Monday and held near 5.13% on 20 Oct with markets pricing an 83% chance of a Fed hold on 28 Oct and a December hike; Brent steadied above $109 with Gulf flows at about 80% of pre-war levels. The rebound did not reach Chinese AI hardware: CXMT fell 7.8% on the mainland and Lenovo 5.8% in Hong Kong on 20 Oct.","src":"Xinhua (US close); Trading Economics (yields, Brent); investinglive","tag":"Markets","title":"Overnight: S&P 500 +0.4% to 7,690.73 and Nasdaq +1.1% to 26,430.89 as chip stocks rebound; Dow −0.1%; US 10-year about 5.13%; Brent near $109.6","url":"https://english.news.cn/northamerica/20261020/e01a276a923546518a3bb0283a6a1897/c.html"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"The draft revision of the technology export catalogue adds 14 prohibited and 26 restricted technologies: 23 in AI, 12 in photovoltaic systems and 5 in biomedicine. Prohibited items include models trained on China's 'core data', high-performance agents for sensitive sectors and offensive cyber AI; restricted items include foundation models above 10^26 FLOPs of training compute or 2 trillion parameters, agents above set complexity thresholds, AI chip design at 14 nm or below, memory-chip design, clusters of more than 10,000 accelerator cards, and cell and gene-therapy process platforms. Open-source releases and API access keep simplified or automatic licensing, and the spokesperson said the revision 'does not target any specific country or region'. A temporary registration requirement for technology sales, licensing and transfers of control applies from 16 Oct, including deals already under way. Wendy Cutler of the Asia Society said the fragility of the US truce 'cannot be overstated' and expects a US response; Sinolytics' Leonhard Xu called the list 'extremely expansive'.","src":"Global Times; Caixin Global; The National; Geopolitechs","tag":"Trade","title":"MOFCOM and the Ministry of Science and Technology propose export controls on 40 AI, solar and biotech technologies; comments to 15 Nov; temporary export registration from 16 Oct","url":"https://www.globaltimes.cn/page/202610/1372418.shtml"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"Bloomberg reported that financial regulators met on 15 Oct on stabilisation measures and that several major insurers were told to report equity holdings, avoid realising losses on underwater positions and align investment with broader economic goals; companies were urged to buy back shares and some investors were guided to curb selling. The Huatai-PineBridge CSI 300 ETF (510300) traded CNY 16.09B on 16 Oct, more than CNY 12.5B of it in the last 20 minutes, after E Fund's 510310 traded CNY 19.4B on 15 Oct and added 4.569B units (about CNY 18.79B); Sina counts more than CNY 140B into broad-based ETFs since late September. The CSRC's 10 Oct symposium and 9 Oct draft cutting the equity-fund registration threshold to CNY 50M from CNY 200M point the same way ahead of Wu Qing's 19 Oct keynote.","src":"Bloomberg via The Edge Malaysia; China Stock Crowd; Sina Finance; BigGo Finance (CSRC symposium)","tag":"Policy","title":"Regulators meet on market stabilisation, give insurers window guidance not to lock in losses, urge the national team to buy faster and ask some investors to slow selling","url":"https://theedgemalaysia.com/node/822106"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"Breadth flipped: 3,061 of 5,215 mainland stocks rose, 61 limit-up against 11 limit-down, the median stock gained 0.45% and turnover fell 4.7% to CNY 1.555T. Defence led (94.9% of 137 stocks up, average +4.18%), property (88.4% up) and autos (15 limit-ups) followed; media and electronics lagged and WuXi AppTec fell 6.6% while Stock Connect seats bought CNY 1.40B of it. The Shenzhen Component slipped 0.12% to 11,802.5, a one-year low. On the week the CSI 300 lost 3.40%, the Shanghai Composite 2.56%, the STAR 50 6.77% and the ChiNext 8.90%, with 12.5% of stocks up. In Hong Kong turnover fell to HK$178.2B, the Hang Seng Tech index rose 1.16% to 4,108, Lenovo rebounded 8%, Longfor rose 6.2%, China Resources Land 3.3%, Alibaba 2.3% and Kuaishou 2.5% on Kling 4.0; the index was down 0.1% on the week. The posture stays Risk-off with 4,217.68 the reversal level.","src":"Tencent Finance klines; China Stock Crowd; BBN Times; The Standard; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"16 Oct close: CSI 300 +0.1% to 4,170.61, still below 4,200; Shanghai +0.2% to 3,716.28; ChiNext −0.4% to 2,772.38, lowest since Aug 2025; Hang Seng +0.5% to 24,186.83, Lenovo +8%","url":"https://chinastockcrowd.substack.com/p/587-of-chinese-stocks-rose-on-friday"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"The 30-year active bond fell 1.75 bp to 2.055%, the 10-year CDB bond to 1.686% and 10-year futures rose 0.11%. Overnight Shibor fell to 1.264%, the lowest since May, and 7-day Shibor to 1.339%; the PBoC lent CNY 60B for seven days at 1.40% against CNY 2B maturing. Analysts say the market reads funding below 1.3% since the holiday as tolerated by the central bank, but regulators named some brokers and banks over bond risk controls and the advice is to take profits into strength. The onshore yuan closed 48 pips firmer at 6.6868 and offshore ended New York at 6.6863, within 1 pip of the 8 July 2022 quote of 6.6862, up about 0.1% on the week; the fixing was 6.7220 against a 6.6947 estimate and the dollar index fell 0.21% to 101.837. Pan Gongsheng pledged in Bangkok to widen foreign access to yuan assets, simplify procedures and push yuan bonds as collateral.","src":"Caixin/CLS via Eastmoney and NetEase; 21st Century Business Herald; Sina FX report; Tradingpedia; Nation Thailand (Pan Gongsheng)","tag":"Markets","title":"10-year CGB falls 1.15 bp to 1.6385%, below 1.65% for the first time since May 2025; yuan closes at 6.6868, firmest since July 2022; PBoC adds CNY 58B net","url":"https://finance.eastmoney.com/a/202610163898611729.html"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"MOFCOM said the claim that EU pressure had worked was 'entirely groundless' and that Brussels 'should not underestimate China's determination and ability to safeguard China's interests'. Von der Leyen confirmed 'a new instrument allowing fast, immediate restrictions on access to the single market', which under certain conditions could fully close the market to Chinese products; it would need 13 of 27 member states and its WTO compatibility is in doubt. Trading Economics cited the EU dispute as a weight on mainland stocks on 16 Oct; the Commission is reviewing import data for surges as a first step toward quotas beyond the 350,000-a-year hybrid-car cap, and the EU deficit with China was a record $34.8B in September.","src":"KuCoin news flash (MOFCOM statement); Seoul Economic Daily; Trading Economics","tag":"Trade","title":"MOFCOM tells the EU not to 'fantasise' about winning concessions as von der Leyen confirms a fast market-access instrument that could close the single market to Chinese goods","url":"https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/china-warns-eu-against-fantasising-on-trade-approach-amid-kill-switch-threat"},{"date":"2026-10-16","impact":"The S&P 500 fell 1.9% on the week and the Nasdaq 4.5%, the Dow slipped 0.3% to 52,002.41 and the Russell 2000 1.7%; the S&P technology sector fell more than 3% while energy rose 2.5% and CoreWeave fell about 12%. Trading Economics shows the US 10-year at 5.09%, down about 14 bp on the week, with markets pricing about an 85% chance of a Fed hold on 28 Oct; the dollar index closed at 101.837 (−0.21%). WTI settled at $101.41 (+3.5%) and Brent at $109.86 (+3.7%), its highest since May, after a Saudi-led coalition intercepted a ballistic missile aimed at Yanbu, Iranian attacks cut Hormuz flows and Ukrainian strikes hit the Ryazan refinery, despite IEA emergency releases of more than 20M barrels. Higher oil feeds China's PPI through refining and extraction prices and lands on Monday's open with the Nasdaq's fall.","src":"AP via ABC News; TheStreet; Trading Economics; EnergyNow; Sina FX report","tag":"Markets","title":"Overnight: S&P 500 −1.1% to 7,659.44 and Nasdaq −2.1% to 26,140.03 in the worst week since June; Brent settles +3.7% at $109.86 after a missile is intercepted toward Yanbu; US 10-year about 5.09%","url":"https://abcnews.com/Business/wireStory/major-us-stock-indexes-fared-friday-10162026-137331951"},{"date":"2026-10-15","impact":"The Shenzhen Component fell 2.43% to 11,816, ChiNext 2.9% and the STAR 50 1.5%; 4,790 of 5,214 stocks fell, the median stock lost 3.0%, all 31 Shenwan industries fell on average and turnover rose 15% to CNY 1.63T. Optical-module stocks fell 5.4%, four shipping stocks closed limit-down after a Reuters report that the US may ease sanctions on shadow-fleet tankers, and big banks (+0.3%) were the only gainers; the CSI 300 ETF 510310 traded CNY 19.4B against CNY 0.8B the day before. In Hong Kong the tech index fell 2.72% to 4,060.99 on HK$224.1B of turnover with Hua Hong −7.8%, MiniMax −12.1%, SMIC −4% and Alibaba −3.7%. UBS's Lei Meng called it a concentrated unwinding of tech crowding plus fund redemptions. The posture turns to Risk-off.","src":"Tencent Finance klines; Xinhua; The Standard; China Stock Crowd; Reuters via Business Recorder","tag":"Markets","title":"15 Oct close: CSI 300 −1.2% to 4,166.01, below the 4,200 line; Shanghai −1.6% to 3,707.48, lowest since August 2025; Hang Seng −1.0% to 24,062.72, Lenovo −14.5%","url":"https://www.thestandard.com.hk/finance/article/345587/Hang-Seng-Index-hovers-at-24000-points-at-close-Lenovo-slumps-14pc"},{"date":"2026-10-14","impact":"TSF stock was CNY 468.17T; the September flow was CNY 3.55T, up CNY 20B y/y, with CNY 1.5T of government bonds, and January to September TSF was CNY 27.46T, CNY 2.63T less than a year earlier. M2 at 7.2% missed the 7.4% consensus and is the slowest since March 2025; M1 slowed to 3.2% from 4.1%. Corporate loans were CNY 1.3T (medium and long-term +890B) and household loans were flat after two months of contraction, with mortgages up CNY 87.4B. Fitch said the late-September package should modestly improve transmission but will not change the property trajectory. Released after the close; the 10-year CGB fell 1.1 bp to 1.646% ahead of it.","src":"PBoC via Caixin Global, Reuters (Investing.com), 24TopNews and Jin10 (KuCoin)","tag":"Data","title":"September credit: new loans CNY 1.08T (consensus 975B) but TSF growth slows to 7.1%, M2 to 7.2% and loan growth to a record-low 4.8%","url":"https://www.caixinglobal.com/2026-10-15/chinas-september-lending-rebounds-beating-forecasts-102493474.html"},{"date":"2026-10-14","impact":"Reuters consensus was 25.5% for exports and 24.5% for imports; August was 25.0% and 28.2%. The surplus is the largest in 20 months, up from $119.1B. Shipments to the US rose to $47.9B from $42.5B in August, integrated-circuit exports rose 136% to $44.95B on DRAM and HBM prices as well as volume, and rare-earth exports were 4,269.8 t, up 6.7% y/y. The figures raise the Q3 GDP arithmetic ahead of the 19 Oct release and add to EU and US pressure to rebalance trade.","src":"GAC via Reuters (Investing.com), AFP via 24NewsHD, AP via ABC News, Startup Fortune/SMM","tag":"Trade","title":"September exports +36.3% y/y to $447.2B, imports +31.0%, surplus $133.5B; exports to the US +39.6%","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/chinas-exports-surge-363-yy-in-september-imports-up-31-4947903"},{"date":"2026-10-14","impact":"PPI beat the 4.5% consensus with a 1.2% m/m rise led by oil extraction (+14.5% m/m), refining (+7.4%), coal mining (+9.5%) and coal processing (+13.6%); coal mining is +35.3% y/y and oil and gas extraction +24.5%. CPI missed the 1.0% consensus, food fell 1.7% y/y with pork −10.0%, services prices fell 0.2% m/m and gasoline rose 16.6% y/y. Downstream prices still fall (autos −2.0%, electricity −3.9%, pharmaceuticals −3.3%), so the print is a margin squeeze, not reflation of demand. Stocks dipped on the release.","src":"NBS via SMM; FXStreet; investinglive; Trading Economics","tag":"Data","title":"September PPI +5.0% y/y, the fastest since June 2022; CPI +0.9% with core at 1.0%","url":"https://news.metal.com/newscontent/104155468-nbs-september-cpi-up-09-yoy-ppi-up-50-yoy-with-both-yoy-growth-rates-widening"}],"headline":"Q3 GDP beat at 4.5% y/y with September retail sales 1.1% and industrial output 5.5%, but the CSI 300 closed only 0.09% higher at 4,174.53 on 19 Oct, still below the 4,200 line, so the posture stays Risk-off; the LPR was held at 3.00% on 20 Oct for a 17th month while Pan Gongsheng flagged a shift to the overnight rate as the main policy rate, a bond-market backstop for non-banks and daily overnight repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct, the 10-year CGB backed up 0.75 bp to 1.645% and the yuan closed at 6.6817, its firmest since July 2022.","marketClose":"Mon 19 Oct 2026 (mainland); Hong Kong closed 19 Oct, last close Fri 16 Oct","notes":["Mainland closes are for Monday 19 Oct: CSI 300 4,174.53, Shanghai Composite 3,730.92 (Tencent daily klines; Xinhua gives 3,730.92, +0.39%, Shenzhen Component 11,817.42 and ChiNext 2,746.29). Hong Kong was closed on 19 Oct for Chung Yeung, so the Hang Seng point is the 16 Oct close of 24,186.83; the 20 Oct open (23,990.05, news.az) and midday level (about 23,960, Trading Economics via TradingView) are intraday and not in the series. Turnover and margin figures are from 21st Century Business Herald; breadth counts for 19 Oct were not available in this run.","September activity data, Q3 GDP, the FAI split, property sales, unemployment and trade in yuan terms are from the NBS English press release of 19 Oct; the consensus figures are from investinglive and Babypips. The 70-city price change of −2.8% y/y and −0.1% m/m is the Reuters calculation (KFGO carry) and FX.co; the NBS release gives only city-tier and city-count detail. Q3 q/q of 1.2% is the seasonally adjusted figure and Q2 q/q was revised to 0.9%.","The 10-year CGB point for 19 Oct is the active bond 260016 at 1.645% from the Eastmoney bond market daily (+0.75 bp); the ChinaBond curve for 19 Oct had not been read. Earlier points: 16 Oct 1.6385% (Caixin/CLS), 15 Oct 1.65% (21st Century Business Herald), 14 Oct ChinaBond curve 1.65%, CFETS series before that.","USD/CNY for 19 Oct is the official 16:30 onshore close of 6.6817 (FX168), 51 pips firmer than 16 Oct. Fixings: 6.7208 on 19 Oct (12 pips firmer, China News Service; Reuters estimate about 6.6897 per Business Recorder) and 6.7200 on 20 Oct (8 pips firmer; investinglive estimate 6.6813). Offshore levels (6.6813 to 6.6818) are Asian-session quotes from Business Recorder and briefs.co, not a New York close.","PBoC operations: 19 Oct CNY 80B seven-day at 1.40% against CNY 50B maturing (Eastmoney, Sohu); 20 Oct zero seven-day operation (China News Service) against CNY 65B maturing per the Sohu weekly schedule; the overnight window of 21 Oct to 2 Nov (up to CNY 1T a day, fixed rate, quantity tender) is from The Paper and investinglive. Shibor fixings are from the Eastmoney bond daily. The overnight rate of 1.25% and DR001 of about 1.26% are from briefs.co; the 1.25% figure dates from June and the rate for the new window has not been published.","Financial Street Forum measures are from Global Times, BigGo Finance, briefs.co, ECNS and ChainCatcher; Pan Gongsheng's remarks are paraphrased in those carries and the only direct analyst quotes are Huatai's \"de facto rate cut\" and ANZ's Xing Zhaopeng. The CICC interpretation on Futu could not be opened. The LPR hold is from Investing.com, Trading Economics and FXStreet.","US figures: index closes from Xinhua; the 10-year (about 5.13%) and Brent ($109.59) are Trading Economics quotes on 20 Oct, not 19 Oct settlements. Copper is the Westmetall LME cash settlement for 19 Oct ($14,496; 16 Oct was $14,595, posted after the last refresh). Iron ore is the Trading Economics 62% Fe CFR quote for 19 Oct.","Consumer confidence for Aug 2026 (89.5) comes from a TrendForce summary dated 1 Oct; Trading Economics still shows July (89.2) and no September reading was found, so August stays provisional. NBS release dates are from its 2026 calendar and are preliminary; the mid-November credit date and the mid-January Q4 GDP date are estimates from the usual release windows."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"The CSI 300 closed at 4,174.53 on 19 Oct, up 0.09% on the GDP beat but below the 4,200 line and the 4,217.68 reversal level for a third session; turnover fell to CNY 1.44T, the ChiNext lost 0.94% to 2,746.29 and the Hang Seng was down about 1% to about 23,960 at midday on 20 Oct after the Chung Yeung holiday, so the posture stays Risk-off until a close above 4,217.68."},"regime":{"label":"Slowdown","note":"Q3 GDP printed 4.5% y/y on 19 Oct (consensus 4.4%, Q2 4.3%) with September retail sales 1.1%, industrial production 5.5% and unemployment 5.2%, so the coincident data stopped deteriorating; the leading set still says slowdown, with NBS new orders flat at 50.5, consumer confidence at 89.5, 70-city prices −2.8% y/y, fixed investment −7.2% year to date and credit growth at 7.1%, and the next test is the October PMIs on 31 Oct."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.3","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":49},{"l":"Nov 25","v":49.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":50.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":49.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":49},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":50.3},{"l":"May 26","v":50},{"l":"Jun 26","v":50.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":49.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":49.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":50.1}],"latest":"50.1","name":"NBS Manufacturing PMI","next":"31 Oct","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"49.8","read":"First reading above 50 since June and in line with consensus. Output rose to 51.7, a nine-month high, while new export orders stayed below 50 at 49.2 and employment fell to 48.4. Input costs jumped to 60.8 from 56.6 and output prices to 54.0, so the survey carries an oil-driven cost push as much as a demand recovery.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Back above 50","src":"NBS via Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/business-confidence/news/587902"},{"change":"−0.1","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":48.8},{"l":"Nov 25","v":49.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":50.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":49.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":48.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":50.6},{"l":"May 26","v":49.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":51.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":48.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":50.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":50.5}],"latest":"50.5","name":"NBS Manufacturing New Orders","next":"31 Oct","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"50.6","read":"Our critical demand signal is above 50 for a second month but did not improve. Foreign orders at 49.2 still contract, so the demand is domestic and partly tied to the September policy push. The gap between orders (50.5) and output (51.7) means inventories, not orders, are absorbing production.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Modest expansion","src":"NBS","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Holding","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202609/t20260901_1965170.html"},{"change":"+0.6","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":50.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":49.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":50.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":50.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":52.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.8},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52.2},{"l":"May 26","v":51.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":50.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":51.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":52.1}],"latest":"52.1","name":"RatingDog (ex-Caixin) Manufacturing PMI","next":"2 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"51.5","read":"Beat the 51.6 forecast with the fastest output growth since April and the strongest new export orders since February. The private survey of smaller exporters has run 1 to 2 points above the official index all year, which fits the export boom: shipments rose 25.0% y/y in August. Services came in at 51.6 and the composite at 52.4.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Tenth month above 50","src":"S&P Global via investinglive","trend":"up","trendWord":"Accelerating","url":"https://investinglive.com/news/ratingdog-china-manufacturing-pmi-52-1-tops-forecast-services-51-6-also-beats/"},{"change":"+1.2","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":50.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":49.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":50.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":49.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":49.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":49.4},{"l":"May 26","v":50.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":50.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":49},{"l":"Aug 26","v":49},{"l":"Sep 26","v":50.2}],"latest":"50.2","name":"NBS Non-Manufacturing PMI","next":"31 Oct","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"49.0","read":"Beat the 49.3 consensus after two months at 49.0. Construction jumped to 50.3 from 46.9, ending an eight-month run of declines, and services rose to 50.2 from 49.3. Real estate and capital-market services stayed below 50, new orders are still weak at 46.5 and employment at 45.9, so the expansion is thin.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Back above 50","src":"NBS via Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebounding","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/non-manufacturing-pmi"},{"change":"+0.3","hist":[{"l":"Aug 25","v":89.6},{"l":"Oct 25","v":89.4},{"l":"Nov 25","v":90.3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":89.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":90.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":91.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":90},{"l":"Apr 26","v":89},{"l":"May 26","v":89.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":89.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":89.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":89.5}],"latest":"89.5","name":"Consumer Confidence Index","next":"Late Oct (Sept reading)","period":"Aug 2026","prior":"89.2","read":"Still more than 10 points under the neutral line and within the 89 to 92 band it has held since 2022; the record low was 85.5 in November 2022. The direction matters more than the level: it has slipped from 91.6 in February to 89.2 in July before a small August uptick. Retail sales at 0.4% y/y say the mood is reaching spending.","ref":100,"refLabel":"100 = neutral line","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Far below 100","src":"NBS via Trading Economics and TrendForce","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"+0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":-2.7},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-3.1},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-3.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-3.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-3.5},{"l":"May 26","v":-3.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-3.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-3.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-2.8}],"latest":"−2.8%","name":"New Home Prices, 70 cities, y/y","next":"16 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"−3.0%","read":"The 39th straight annual decline but the smallest in nine months, with prices down 0.1% m/m for a fourth month (Reuters calculation). New-home prices rose m/m in 17 of 70 cities, two more than in August, and second-hand prices in 9 (3 in August). Tier-1 new-home prices were −0.5% y/y after −0.9% and tier-1 second-hand −1.5% after −2.7%: Shanghai +2.8% y/y, Shenzhen −1.0% (−2.3%), Guangzhou −1.1% (−1.9%) and Beijing −2.7% (−2.3%). Property investment fell 20.6% year to date and new-home sales 13.2% by area, so the price narrowing has not reached volumes; October prices are due 16 Nov.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = flat prices","signal":"warn","signalWord":"39th monthly fall","src":"NBS via Xinhua, Reuters (KFGO carry) and FX.co","trend":"up","trendWord":"Decline narrowing","url":"https://english.news.cn/20261019/b53a637a2f0f462abb75d08194abc399/c.html"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"+0.3 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":6.5},{"l":"Oct 25","v":4.9},{"l":"Nov 25","v":4.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4.1},{"l":"May 26","v":4.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":5.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":5.5}],"latest":"+5.5%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"16 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+5.2%","read":"Beat the 5.4% forecast and the fastest since March, with output up 0.61% m/m. January to September output is up 5.3%, with equipment manufacturing +10.2% and high-tech manufacturing +14.3%, and September exports rose 29.6% in yuan terms, so production still tracks foreign demand. Industrial purchasing prices rose 6.9% y/y in September against PPI at 5.0%, which is the margin squeeze the 27 Oct profits release will show.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Export-led","src":"NBS press release (19 Oct) via stats.gov.cn; ActionForex","trend":"up","trendWord":"Fastest in six months","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202610/t20261019_1965619.html"},{"change":"+0.7 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":3},{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-0.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.6},{"l":"Aug 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.1}],"latest":"+1.1%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"16 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+0.4%","read":"Beat the 0.5% to 1.0% forecasts and the fastest since March, up 0.35% m/m, but the first nine months are up only 1.1% for goods against 5.0% for services retail. September passenger-car retail sales were down 28.0% y/y (CPCA), so the gain came from the September subsidy push and services, not autos. Consumer confidence at 89.5 and core CPI at 1.0% say the household side has not turned; October retail sales are due 16 Nov.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Still near 1%","src":"NBS press release (19 Oct) via stats.gov.cn; investinglive","trend":"up","trendWord":"Fastest since March","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202610/t20261019_1965619.html"},{"change":"0.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":-0.5},{"l":"Oct 25","v":-1.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-3.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-1.6},{"l":"May 26","v":-4.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-5.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-6.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-7.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-7.2}],"latest":"−7.2%","name":"Fixed Asset Investment, YTD y/y","next":"16 Nov","period":"Jan-Sep 2026","prior":"−7.2%","read":"Unchanged at −7.2% year to date against a −7.3% forecast, the first month since February in which the decline did not deepen; September investment rose 0.07% m/m. Property development investment is down 20.6% (from −19.9%), infrastructure −4.2% (−4.0%) and manufacturing −2.1% (−2.3%), with private investment −10.4% and high-tech investment +4.9%, so the stabilisation is in manufacturing while property and infrastructure still weaken. The CNY 550B local debt quota released on 9 Oct is the only new infrastructure money; October data are due 16 Nov.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = flat investment","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Contracting","src":"NBS press release (19 Oct) via stats.gov.cn","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Stopped deepening","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202610/t20261019_1965619.html"},{"change":"+11.3 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":8.3},{"l":"Oct 25","v":-1.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":5.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":21.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":14.1},{"l":"May 26","v":19.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":27},{"l":"Jul 26","v":23.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":25},{"l":"Sep 26","v":36.3}],"latest":"+36.3%","name":"Exports, y/y","next":"10 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+25.0%","read":"Exports were $447.18B in September, up 36.3% y/y against a 25.5% consensus, the strongest growth since February; imports rose 31.0% and the surplus was $133.53B, the largest in 20 months. Shipments to the US rose 39.6% to $47.9B from $42.5B in August under the tariff truce, and integrated-circuit exports rose 136% to $44.95B, lifted by DRAM and HBM prices as well as volume. Rare-earth exports were 4,269.8 t, up 6.7% y/y but down 9.8% m/m; October trade is due 10 Nov.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Strongest since Feb","src":"GAC via Trading Economics; Reuters via Investing.com; AFP via 24NewsHD; SMM","trend":"up","trendWord":"Surging","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/exports-yoy"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":5.2},{"l":"Oct 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Feb 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":5.2},{"l":"May 26","v":5.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":5.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":5.2}],"latest":"5.2%","name":"Surveyed Urban Unemployment","next":"16 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"5.3%","read":"Fell 0.1 point to 5.2% on the usual post-summer dip, matching September 2025 and the January to September average of 5.2%; the 31-city rate was also 5.2% and migrant workers with rural registration 5.0%. Both PMI employment indexes are still below 50 (48.4 manufacturing, 45.9 non-manufacturing) and the services share of employment is 49.4%. The rate sits mid-way in the 5.0% to 5.4% range of the past three years.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Same as a year ago","src":"NBS press release (19 Oct) via stats.gov.cn; Xinhua","trend":"down","trendWord":"Seasonal dip","url":"https://english.news.cn/20261019/dcf90586aacb4f79b331d22661c704d4/c.html"},{"change":"+0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q4 23","v":5.2},{"l":"Q1 24","v":5.3},{"l":"Q2 24","v":4.7},{"l":"Q3 24","v":4.6},{"l":"Q4 24","v":5.4},{"l":"Q1 25","v":5.4},{"l":"Q2 25","v":5.2},{"l":"Q3 25","v":4.8},{"l":"Q4 25","v":4.5},{"l":"Q1 26","v":5},{"l":"Q2 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Q3 26","v":4.5}],"latest":"+4.5%","name":"Real GDP, y/y","next":"Mid-Jan","period":"Q3 2026","prior":"+4.3%","read":"Beat the 4.4% Reuters consensus with 1.2% q/q (0.9% in Q2, consensus 1.1%), taking January to September to 4.6% and nominal GDP to CNY 107.1T; the secondary sector grew 3.8% year to date and services 5.2%. Growth is at the bottom of the 4.5% to 5.0% target range and rests on exports (September +29.6% in yuan terms) and high-tech output, with the NBS calling domestic demand \"insufficient\"; Capital Economics says the September data show the economy \"regained momentum\" while ANZ says fiscal support remains crucial. Q4 GDP is due in mid-January; a Q4 print at 4.4% or better delivers the year.","ref":5,"refLabel":"5% = top of 4.5-5% target","signal":"good","signalWord":"Bottom of target range","src":"NBS press release (19 Oct) via stats.gov.cn; investinglive; Business Recorder (Reuters)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Re-accelerating","url":"https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202610/t20261019_1965619.html"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":0.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":0.7},{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.2},{"l":"May 26","v":1.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":0.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.9}],"latest":"+0.9%","name":"CPI, y/y","next":"9 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+0.8%","read":"Below the 1.0% consensus and up 0.2% m/m. Core CPI held at 1.0% y/y, food fell 1.7% y/y with pork down 10.0%, and services prices fell 0.2% m/m as airfares dropped 8.7% and hotel rates 7.0% after the summer. Energy inflation rose to 7.3% from 4.1% with gasoline up 16.6% y/y, so the headline is oil, not demand; October CPI is due 9 Nov.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = deflation line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 1%","src":"NBS via SMM and FXStreet","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://news.metal.com/newscontent/104155468-nbs-september-cpi-up-09-yoy-ppi-up-50-yoy-with-both-yoy-growth-rates-widening"},{"change":"+1.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-2.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-1.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-1.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-0.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.8},{"l":"May 26","v":3.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":5}],"latest":"+5.0%","name":"PPI, y/y","next":"9 Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+3.8%","read":"Above the 4.5% consensus and the fastest since June 2022, with a 1.2% m/m rise after 0.4% in August. The drivers are cost-push: oil extraction +14.5% m/m, refining +7.4%, coal mining +9.5% and coal processing +13.6%; by sector, coal mining is +35.3% y/y and oil and gas extraction +24.5%. Downstream prices still fall (autos −2.0% y/y, electricity −3.9%, pharmaceuticals −3.3%), so margins are squeezed rather than demand recovering; September industrial profits on 27 Oct show the effect.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = deflation line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since 2022","src":"NBS via SMM and FXStreet","trend":"up","trendWord":"Jumping","url":"https://news.metal.com/newscontent/104155468-nbs-september-cpi-up-09-yoy-ppi-up-50-yoy-with-both-yoy-growth-rates-widening"},{"change":"−10 bp (May 2025)","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3},{"l":"20 Oct","v":3}],"latest":"3.00%","name":"1-Year Loan Prime Rate","next":"20 Nov","period":"Since 20 May 2025","prior":"3.10%","read":"Held at 3.00% on 20 Oct for a 17th month; the 5-year rate is 3.50%. The hold was unanimous in the polls after the 4.5% GDP print, and Pan Gongsheng said on 19 Oct that the deposit and lending benchmark rates, unchanged since October 2015, will be retired once the remaining judicial uses move to market rates, while the LPR keeps guiding loans to smaller firms and households. The next fixing is 20 Nov; the proposed switch to the overnight rate as the main policy rate is the more important change.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"17 months unchanged","src":"Bank of China LPR table","trend":"flat","trendWord":"On hold","url":"https://www.boc.cn/fimarkets/lilv/fd32/201310/t20131031_2591219.html"},{"change":"0 bp","hist":[],"latest":"1.40%","name":"7-Day Reverse Repo Rate","period":"20 Oct 2026","prior":"1.40%","read":"The PBoC lent CNY 80B for seven days at 1.40% on 19 Oct against CNY 50B maturing (CNY 30B net) and ran a zero seven-day operation on 20 Oct against CNY 65B maturing, citing dealer demand; it then announced daily overnight reverse repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct to 2 Nov at a fixed rate, covering the 26 Oct tax date. Overnight Shibor held at 1.264% and 7-day Shibor fell to 1.322%, the lowest since May. Pan Gongsheng said on 19 Oct the bank will study switching its main open-market tenor from seven days to overnight, making the overnight repo rate (new issues 1.25%, DR001 about 1.26%) the main policy rate; Huatai calls that a de facto cut and ANZ sees 1.25% as a plausible level.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Record low, unchanged","src":"PBoC via China News Service and The Paper (20 Oct); Eastmoney bond close (19 Oct); briefs.co and BigGo (Pan Gongsheng remarks)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"On hold","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/reverse-repo-rate"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":8.5},{"l":"Nov 25","v":8.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":8.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":7.9},{"l":"Apr 26","v":7.8},{"l":"May 26","v":7.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":7.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":7.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":7.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":7.1}],"latest":"7.1%","name":"Total Social Financing, stock y/y","next":"Mid-Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"7.2%","read":"TSF stock was CNY 468.17T at end-September, up 7.1% y/y, down from 7.2% in August and the slowest growth since mid-2025. The September flow was CNY 3.55T, up CNY 20B y/y, carried by CNY 1.5T of government bonds; January to September TSF was CNY 27.46T, CNY 2.63T less than a year earlier. New loans of CNY 1.08T beat the CNY 975B consensus on a quarter-end push, but loan stock growth hit a record-low 4.8% and household loans only stopped falling.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"14th month slower","src":"PBoC via 24TopNews, Caixin Global and Reuters (Investing.com)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Decelerating","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/china-september-new-bank-loans-jump-more-than-expected-on-stimulus-seasonal-boost-4948504"},{"change":"−0.3 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":8.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":8.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":8.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":8.6},{"l":"May 26","v":8.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":7.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":7.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":7.2}],"latest":"7.2%","name":"M2, y/y","next":"Mid-Nov","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"7.5%","read":"M2 grew 7.2% y/y to CNY 359.6T in September, down 0.3 points from August and below the 7.4% consensus, the slowest since March 2025. M1 slowed to 3.2% from 4.1%, so the M1-M2 gap widened again, which under the framework says cash is not turning into activity. The slowdown comes despite the 29 Sept easing package and ahead of the CNY 700B six-month outright reverse repo on 15 Oct.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"18-month low","src":"PBoC via Caixin Global, Reuters (Investing.com) and Jin10 (KuCoin)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://www.caixinglobal.com/2026-10-15/chinas-september-lending-rebounds-beating-forecasts-102493474.html"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added when the China page was built. They are not part of our original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"+0.75 bp on 19 Oct","hist":[{"l":"14 Sep","v":1.686},{"l":"18 Sep","v":1.6825},{"l":"24 Sep","v":1.6739},{"l":"30 Sep","v":1.675},{"l":"9 Oct","v":1.6867},{"l":"12 Oct","v":1.6745},{"l":"13 Oct","v":1.67},{"l":"14 Oct","v":1.65},{"l":"15 Oct","v":1.65},{"l":"16 Oct","v":1.6385},{"l":"19 Oct","v":1.645}],"latest":"1.645%","name":"10-Year CGB Yield","period":"19 Oct (active bond at close)","prior":"1.6385% on 16 Oct (active bond)","read":"The active 10-year bond 260016 rose 0.75 bp to 1.645% on 19 Oct after the GDP beat, the 30-year 260004 rose 1.4 bp to 2.069% and the 10-year CDB bond 0.25 bp to 1.6885%; 30-year futures fell 0.22% and 10-year futures 0.01%. Funding stayed loose with overnight Shibor at 1.264% and 7-day Shibor at 1.322%, the lowest since May, and the PBoC added CNY 30B net. The market is now weighing the overnight-rate framework (Guosheng sees the 10-year near 1.6% and the 30-year below 2.0%) against a CNY 25B 30-year auction on 23 Oct and the tax date on 26 Oct; the spread to the US 10-year (about 5.13%) is about 348 bp.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Near record low","src":"Eastmoney bond market daily (active bond, 19 Oct); Caixin/CLS (16 Oct); ChinaBond and CFETS series for earlier points","trend":"up","trendWord":"Backed up from the low","url":"https://yield.chinabond.com.cn/cbweb-czb-web/czb/moreInfo?locale=en_US&nameType=1"},{"change":"−4.2%","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":4526.66},{"l":"Dec 25","v":4629.94},{"l":"Jan 26","v":4706.34},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4710.65},{"l":"Mar 26","v":4450.05},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4807.31},{"l":"May 26","v":4892.12},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4979.43},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4588.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4625.09},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4357.62},{"l":"19 Oct","v":4174.53}],"latest":"4,174.53","name":"CSI 300","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"4,357.62 on 30 Sep","read":"The CSI 300 rose 0.09% to 4,174.53 on 19 Oct in a 4,143.66 to 4,192.02 range, 4.2% below the 30 Sept close and below the 4,200 posture line and the 4,217.68 reversal level for a third session, so the posture stays Risk-off. The GDP beat lifted banks (ICBC and China Construction Bank at intraday records, four banks up more than 3%), liquor and robotics (Unitree +10%), while the ChiNext fell 0.94% to 2,746.29, the STAR 50 about 2% and CXMT 7.8% as the AI hardware unwind continued; turnover fell to CNY 1.44T from 1.56T and the margin balance was CNY 2.49T after a CNY 60B drop last week. CITIC says the correction ends when institutional holdings regain excess returns and CICC puts the index at about 13 times earnings; a close above 4,217.68 reverses the posture and a close below the 4,115.07 low of 15 Oct extends it.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Below 4,200","src":"Tencent Finance daily kline; Xinhua; 21st Century Business Herald; Business Recorder (Reuters)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"+0.09% on GDP day","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/stock-market"},{"change":"−1.7%","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":25858.89},{"l":"Dec 25","v":25630.54},{"l":"Jan 26","v":27387.11},{"l":"Feb 26","v":26630.54},{"l":"Mar 26","v":24788.14},{"l":"Apr 26","v":25776.53},{"l":"May 26","v":25182.39},{"l":"Jun 26","v":22881.02},{"l":"Jul 26","v":25884.43},{"l":"Aug 26","v":25566.99},{"l":"Sep 26","v":24613.27},{"l":"16 Oct","v":24186.83}],"latest":"24,186.83","name":"Hang Seng","period":"16 Oct close (market closed 19 Oct)","prior":"24,613.27 on 30 Sep","read":"Hong Kong was closed for Chung Yeung on 19 Oct, so the last close is 24,186.83 from 16 Oct (+0.52% on HK$178.2B of turnover, Lenovo +8%, Hang Seng Tech +1.16%). On 20 Oct the index opened at 23,990.05 (−0.81%) and was about 1% lower near 23,960 at midday with retail −2.0%, Lenovo −5.8%, Innovent −4.4%, Tencent −2.0% and SMIC −1.5%, as the LPR hold and the AI-financing worries outweighed the GDP beat; the 20 Oct close will be in the next refresh. The index is 1.7% below the 30 Sept close against 4.2% for the CSI 300.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Testing 24,000","src":"Tencent Finance daily kline; news.az (20 Oct open); Trading Economics via TradingView (20 Oct midday); The Standard","trend":"down","trendWord":"Reopened about −1%","url":"https://www.thestandard.com.hk/finance/article/345687/Hong-Kong-shares-close-higher-Lenovo-up-8-percent"},{"change":"−2.9%","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3888.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3968.84},{"l":"Jan 26","v":4117.95},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4162.88},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3891.86},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4112.16},{"l":"May 26","v":4068.57},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4094.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3832.26},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3986.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3842.19},{"l":"19 Oct","v":3730.92}],"latest":"3,730.92","name":"Shanghai Composite","period":"19 Oct close","prior":"3,842.19 on 30 Sep","read":"The Shanghai Composite rose 14.64 points (0.39%) to 3,730.92 on 19 Oct after opening at 3,694.12, in a 3,692.29 to 3,743.80 range, and is 2.9% below the 30 Sept close of 3,842.19. Banks (ICBC and CCB at intraday records), liquor, robotics and defence led while agriculture, property, retail and batteries fell; the Shenzhen Component rose 0.13% to 11,817.42 and the ChiNext fell 0.94% to 2,746.29 after dropping more than 2% intraday. Turnover of CNY 1.44T was down CNY 118B on the day, so the index held 3,700 on lighter volume with the state-linked sectors doing the work.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Held 3,700","src":"Tencent Finance daily kline; Xinhua; 21st Century Business Herald","trend":"up","trendWord":"+0.39%, banks led","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/stock-market"},{"change":"−0.34% since 30 Sep","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":7.0748},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.9876},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6.952},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.861},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.8933},{"l":"Apr 26","v":6.8266},{"l":"May 26","v":6.7662},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.7854},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6.7528},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.7202},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6.7045},{"l":"19 Oct","v":6.6817}],"latest":"6.6817","name":"USD/CNY","period":"19 Oct","prior":"6.7045 on 30 Sep","read":"The onshore yuan closed at 6.6817 on 19 Oct, 51 pips firmer than 16 Oct and the strongest close since July 2022, after the GDP beat; offshore traded 6.6813 to 6.6818. The PBoC set the fixing at 6.7208 on 19 Oct (12 pips firmer, the strongest since 2 February 2023 but 311 pips weaker than the Reuters estimate) and 6.7200 on 20 Oct against a 6.6813 estimate, so the fix still sits almost 400 pips weaker than spot and the bank is slowing rather than stopping the move. Huatai Futures expects appreciation to slow, MUFG says the 4.5% growth print supports the yuan but weak household demand caps it, and the dollar index was about 101.8; 6.65 is the next marker and 7.0 is far away.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Strongest since July 2022","src":"CFETS 16:30 close via FX168 (19 Oct); China News Service (fixings 19 and 20 Oct); Business Recorder (Reuters); investinglive (20 Oct estimate)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Yuan at a four-year high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/china/currency"},{"change":"+0.1% since 30 Sep","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":11004},{"l":"Dec 25","v":12504},{"l":"Jan 26","v":13369.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":13439.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":12160},{"l":"Apr 26","v":13015.5},{"l":"May 26","v":13615},{"l":"Jun 26","v":13341},{"l":"Jul 26","v":13834},{"l":"Aug 26","v":14535},{"l":"Sep 26","v":14487},{"l":"19 Oct","v":14496}],"latest":"$14,496","name":"Copper, LME","period":"19 Oct cash settlement","prior":"$14,487 on 30 Sep","read":"LME cash copper settled at $14,496/t on 19 Oct, down $99 (0.7%) from $14,595 on 16 Oct, with three-month at $14,378; the cash premium over three-month is $118, so nearby supply is still tight. The price is about flat since 30 Sept and within 3% of the $14,875 record, which keeps copper as a supportive gauge for the export and manufacturing side, while iron ore at $90.95 is down 6.7% in a month and tracks the property data (investment −20.6% year to date). Chinese September data (industrial production +5.5%, FAI −7.2%) fit that split.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Demand proxy still high","src":"Westmetall (LME cash settlement and 3-month, 19 Oct)","trend":"down","trendWord":"−0.7% on 19 Oct","url":"https://www.westmetall.com/en/markdaten.php?action=table&field=LME_Cu_cash"},{"change":"−6.7% in a month","hist":[],"latest":"$90.95/t","name":"Iron Ore","period":"19 Oct","prior":"$90.90/t on 16 Oct","read":"Iron ore was $90.95/t on 19 Oct, up 0.06% on the day but down 6.73% in a month and 13.79% y/y on the Trading Economics 62% Fe quote. The September data confirmed the weak end-use picture: property investment −20.6% year to date, infrastructure −4.2%, new-home sales −13.2% by area and 70-city prices −2.8% y/y, against industrial production +5.5%. The CNY 550B local debt quota released on 9 Oct and the PBoC liquidity operations are the only infrastructure support in the pipeline; property and agriculture stocks fell on the mainland on 19 Oct despite the narrower price decline.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Construction demand weak","src":"Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Steady near $91","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/iron-ore"}],"sub":"Additions to the leading and coincident core. Closes for Monday 19 Oct in the mainland, copper and iron ore; Hong Kong was closed on 19 Oct, so the Hang Seng is the 16 Oct close and 20 Oct figures in the text are midday levels.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["The data beat but did not change the structure. Q3 GDP rose 4.5% y/y (consensus 4.4%, Q2 4.3%) and 1.2% q/q, putting January to September at 4.6% against the 4.5% to 5.0% target. September industrial production rose 5.5% (August 5.2%, the fastest in six months), retail sales 1.1% (0.4%, the fastest since March), the services production index 4.8% and the surveyed unemployment rate fell to 5.2% from 5.3%. Fixed-asset investment stayed at −7.2% year to date with property investment −20.6%, infrastructure −4.2% and manufacturing −2.1%; new-home sales fell 13.2% by floor space and 13.9% by value. The NBS said domestic demand remains \"insufficient\".","Property is falling more slowly. 70-city new home prices fell 2.8% y/y in September after −3.0% in August, the 39th monthly decline but the smallest in nine months, and 0.1% m/m; prices rose m/m in 17 cities (15 in August), tier-1 new-home prices were −0.5% y/y (−0.9%) and tier-1 second-hand prices −1.5% (−2.7%). Shanghai is +2.8% y/y, Shenzhen −1.0% and Guangzhou −1.1%, while Beijing slipped to −2.7% from −2.3%. The NBS said the market is \"moving in a positive direction\"; the investment and sales data say the adjustment is not over.","Markets took the beat as less stimulus, not more growth. The CSI 300 rose 0.09% to 4,174.53 on 19 Oct in a 4,143.66 to 4,192.02 range and the Shanghai Composite 0.39% to 3,730.92 as banks (ICBC and CCB at intraday records) and liquor led, while the ChiNext fell 0.94% to 2,746.29, the STAR 50 about 2% and CXMT 7.8%; turnover fell to CNY 1.44T from 1.56T and the margin balance was CNY 2.49T after a CNY 60B fall last week. Hong Kong was closed for Chung Yeung on 19 Oct and reopened about 1% lower on 20 Oct near 23,960, with Lenovo −5.8% and Tencent −2.0%, after the LPR hold and the Nasdaq's 1.1% Monday rebound.","The PBoC used the Financial Street Forum for framework changes rather than rate moves. Pan Gongsheng said the bank will study switching its main open-market tenor from seven days (1.40%) to overnight (new issues at 1.25%, DR001 about 1.26%), which Huatai reads as a de facto rate cut; announced a liquidity backstop for large redemptions at wealth-management subsidiaries and fund managers, SLF access for all deposit-takers including foreign banks, and a pilot for eight offshore clearing banks in the onshore FX market; and on 20 Oct scheduled daily overnight reverse repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct to 2 Nov, covering the 26 Oct tax date. The 7-day operation on 20 Oct was zero against CNY 65B maturing after CNY 80B (CNY 30B net) on 19 Oct, and the LPR was held at 3.00% and 3.50%. Wu Qing offered 18 actively managed ETFs, buy-back encouragement and a warning on offshore leveraged A-share products, not direct buying.","Bonds and the yuan read the same print differently. The 10-year active bond rose 0.75 bp to 1.645% and the 30-year 1.4 bp to 2.069% on 19 Oct as the data beat and a CNY 25B 30-year auction on 23 Oct weighed, while overnight Shibor held at 1.264% and 7-day Shibor fell to 1.322%, the lowest since May. The onshore yuan closed 51 pips firmer at 6.6817, the strongest since July 2022, with the fixing at 6.7208 on 19 Oct (311 pips weaker than the Reuters estimate) and 6.7200 on 20 Oct against a 6.6813 estimate, so the PBoC is still slowing the move. LME cash copper fell to $14,496 on 19 Oct, iron ore held at $90.95, Brent is near $109.6 and the US 10-year about 5.13% ahead of the 28 Oct FOMC."],"translation":[{"body":"The CSI 300 closed at 4,174.53 on 19 Oct, up 0.09% on the GDP beat but below the 4,200 line and the 4,217.68 reversal level for a third session, so the posture stays Risk-off; the 15 Oct low of 4,115.07 is the level that extends the call and the 19 Oct low was 4,143.66. Under the framework the coincident data improved: GDP 4.5%, retail sales 1.1%, industrial production 5.5% and unemployment 5.2%, with PMIs above 50 and exports up 36.3%. The leading and credit signals did not: new orders flat at 50.5, consumer confidence 89.5, home prices −2.8%, FAI −7.2% with property −20.6%, TSF 7.1% and M1 3.2%. The market read the beat as less stimulus: banks and liquor rose, the ChiNext fell 0.94% and the STAR 50 about 2% as the AI hardware unwind continued (CXMT −7.8%), turnover fell to CNY 1.44T and the margin balance fell CNY 60B last week to CNY 2.49T. Wu Qing offered 18 active ETFs, buy-back encouragement and a warning on offshore leveraged products rather than new direct buying, and the LPR hold on 20 Oct removed the rate-cut hope, so Hong Kong reopened about 1% lower near 23,960 with Lenovo −5.8% and Tencent −2.0%. A market that cannot rise on a beat with falling turnover is still a supported floor rather than a base; the next catalysts are the 27 Oct industrial profits, the 28 Oct FOMC and the 31 Oct PMIs.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"The 10-year active bond rose 0.75 bp to 1.645% on 19 Oct and the 30-year 1.4 bp to 2.069%, a small back-up from the lowest level since May 2025 as GDP beat and a CNY 25B 30-year auction on 23 Oct came into view; the US 10-year is about 5.13%, so the gap is about 348 bp and there is no carry. The bigger change is the framework: Pan Gongsheng said the PBoC will study making the overnight repo (new issues at 1.25%, DR001 about 1.26%) its main policy rate instead of the 7-day at 1.40%, Huatai calls that a de facto cut, ANZ sees 1.25% as plausible and Guosheng targets the 10-year near 1.6% and the 30-year below 2.0%. Funding supports that view, with overnight Shibor at 1.264% and 7-day Shibor at 1.322%, the lowest since May, and the PBoC has scheduled daily overnight reverse repos of up to CNY 1T from 21 Oct to 2 Nov across the 26 Oct tax date; the new liquidity backstop for non-bank redemptions caps the tail risk of a bond selloff loop. The brake is still supply and regulators naming brokers over bond risk, so a test of 1.6% is possible without a sustained break. The yuan closed at 6.6817 on 19 Oct, the firmest since July 2022, with fixings at 6.7208 and 6.7200 set 300 to 400 pips weaker than estimates, so the PBoC is slowing the move while the $133.5B September surplus pushes it; the pilot for eight offshore clearing banks in the onshore FX market adds two-way flow. 6.65 is the next marker.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"The LPR was held at 3.00% and 3.50% on 20 Oct for a 17th month, in line with every forecast after the 4.5% GDP print, and the PBoC kept the 7-day rate at 1.40%; it lent CNY 80B on 19 Oct (CNY 30B net) and ran a zero 7-day operation on 20 Oct against CNY 65B maturing while announcing overnight repos of up to CNY 1T a day from 21 Oct to 2 Nov. The Financial Street Forum delivered structure rather than stimulus: a study of the overnight rate as the main policy rate, retirement of the 2015 benchmark deposit and lending rates, a liquidity backstop for large redemptions at wealth-management subsidiaries and fund managers built with the NFRA and CSRC, SLF access for all deposit-takers including foreign banks, interbank-exchange bond market connection once testing ends, a pilot for eight offshore yuan clearing banks in onshore FX trading, and guidance on financing the three innovation centres. The CSRC approved 18 actively managed ETFs, encouraged buy-backs and major-shareholder purchases and warned about offshore leveraged A-share products; SAFE promised current-account easing. None of it adds fiscal demand: FAI is −7.2%, property investment −20.6% and the IMF's view that fiscal support is too small stands. The Fifth Plenum on 26 to 29 Oct is about party governance, the 27 Oct profits data show whether PPI at 5.0% is a margin squeeze, and October credit data in mid-November test whether cheaper overnight money is borrowed.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
